In a parallel chip-firing game on CnC_{n}, if ct​(v)>1c_{t}(v)>1 for any vv, then T=1T=1.
Proof.
Suppose ct​(v0)>1c_{t}(v_{0})>1. Note that if ct​(v)≥1c_{t}(v)\geq 1 then ct+1​(v)≤ct​(v)c_{t+1}(v)\leq c_{t}(v), so we must have 1<ct​(v0)=ct+1​(v0)=⋯=ct+T−1​(v0)=ct+T​(v0)1<c_{t}(v_{0})=c_{t+1}(v_{0})=\dots=c_{t+T-1}(v_{0})=c_{t+T}(v_{0}). We conclude that v0v_{0} must fire on every round and so v1v_{1} must fire on every round as well. By induction, we conclude that every vertex fires on every round, so T=1T=1.
∎
We now prove our main theorem about directed cycles.
Theorem 3.5.
The possible periods TT of a parallel chip-firing game on CnC_{n} are T=iT=i for all ii such that i|n.i\;|\;n.
Proof.
Consider a parallel chip-firing game on CnC_{n} with period TT. If T=1T=1, we have T|nT\;|\;n. Otherwise, by Lemma 3.4, we have ct​(v)≤1c_{t}(v)\leq 1 for all vv and tt. We conclude that ct​(v)=Ft​(v)c_{t}(v)=F_{t}(v) for all vv and tt.
Note that if Ft​(vi)=1,F_{t}(v_{i})=1, then vi−1v_{i-1} has one chip after round t,t, so Ft+1​(vi−1)=1.F_{t+1}(v_{i-1})=1. Thus, if Ft​(v)=1,F_{t}(v)=1, then Ft+n​(v)=1.F_{t+n}(v)=1. Additionally, if Ft+n​(v)=1,F_{t+n}(v)=1, then Ft+n​T​(v)=1F_{t+nT}(v)=1, and by the definition of a period, Ft​(v)=1F_{t}(v)=1 as well. Therefore, Ft​(v)=Ft+n​(v)F_{t}(v)=F_{t+n}(v) for all vv and tt, so ct​(v)=ct+n​(v)c_{t}(v)=c_{t+n}(v) for all vv and tt. Thus, we must have T|n.T\;|\;n.
To construct a parallel chip-firing game with period T=iT=i for any i|n,i\;|\;n, place a single chip on each vertex vjv_{j} that satisfies j≡0(modi)j\equiv 0\pmod{i}. On round t,t, there will be a chip on each vertex vjv_{j} that satisfies j≡−t(modi).j\equiv-t\pmod{i}. The smallest TT for which cv​(t)=cv​(t+T)c_{v}(t)=c_{v}(t+T) is therefore T=iT=i.
∎
3.3 Complete Graphs
We first note as a surprising aside that while there exists a parallel chip-firing game on any undirected graph with T=2T=2, it is possible for there to be no orientations of a graph that allow for a parallel chip-firing game with T=2T=2. We begin by citing a lemma from Jiang Jiang (2010).
Lemma 3.6 ((Jiang, 2010, Proposition 2.5)).
For any parallel chip-firing game on an undirected graph, cT​(v)=c0​(v)c_{T}(v)=c_{0}(v) for all v∈Vv\in V if and only if ∑t=0T−1Ft​(v)\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}F_{t}(v) is constant for all v∈Vv\in V.
In Lemma 6.1, we show that there exists a parallel chip-firing game on any undirected graph GG such that every vertex fires exactly once in the first 2 rounds. However, parallel chip-firing games on directed graphs behave differently, as demonstrated by the following lemma.
Lemma 3.7.
No parallel chip-firing games on any orientation of KnK_{n} with n≤4n\leq 4 have T=2T=2.
Proof.
Note that K2K_{2} is a path, and K3K_{3} is a cycle. For these cases, see Corollary 3.2 and Theorem 3.5, respectively. Then consider a parallel chip-firing game on a strongly connected orientation D=(V,E)D=(V,\,E) of K4K_{4} with T=2T=2. Recall that because DD is strongly connected, each vertex vv in DD must satisfy deg+⁡v>0\deg^{+}v>0 and deg−⁡v>0\deg^{-}v>0. Also, by Lemma 2.2, each vertex must fire at least once in each period.
Since deg⁡v=3\deg v=3, vv must be one of two types: deg−⁡v=1\deg^{-}v=1 and deg+⁡v=2\deg^{+}v=2, or deg−⁡v=2\deg^{-}v=2 and deg+⁡v=1\deg^{+}v=1. Additionally, since ∑v∈Vdeg−⁡v=∑v∈Vdeg+⁡v\sum_{v\in V}\deg^{-}v=\sum_{v\in V}\deg^{+}v, there must be two vertices of each type. Let deg+⁡v1=deg+⁡v2=1\deg^{+}v_{1}=\deg^{+}v_{2}=1, and let deg+⁡u1=deg+⁡u2=2\deg^{+}u_{1}=\deg^{+}u_{2}=2.
Note that there must exist an edge between v1v_{1} and v2v_{2} and that regardless of its direction, one of the two vertices must view it as its single out-edge. Thus, there is only one edge directed from v1v_{1} or v2v_{2} to u1u_{1} or u2u_{2}. Without loss of generality, let it be directed from v1v_{1} to u1u_{1}. On the other hand, there are three edges directed from u1u_{1} or u2u_{2} to v1v_{1} or v2v_{2}.
Since T=2T=2, we must have fv1≤2f_{v_{1}}\leq 2. However, fu1≥1f_{u_{1}}\geq 1 and fu2≥1f_{u_{2}}\geq 1. Then there must be at most two chips that travel from a vertex in V={v1,v2}V=\{v_{1},\,v_{2}\} to one in U={u1,u2}U=\{u_{1},\,u_{2}\}, but at least three chips that travel from a vertex in UU to one in VV. It is impossible for every vertex to have the same number of chips after 2 rounds, so T≠2T\neq 2 for all parallel chip-firing games on DD.
∎
Next, let τ⁡(n)\tau(n) be the maximum possible period of a parallel chip-firing game on an orientation of KnK_{n}. We show that τ⁡(n)\tau(n) is at least asymptotic to (n−1)!(n-1)!. We conjecture that τ⁡(n)\tau(n) is indeed asymptotic to (n−1)!(n-1)!
Conjecture 3.8.
It holds that τ⁡(n)=Θ⁡((n−1)!)\tau(n)=\Theta((n-1)!).
We define the following orientation of KnK_{n} with vertices v1,v2,…,vnv_{1},\,v_{2},\,\ldots,\,v_{n} to be a useful orientation.
•
There exist edges directed from viv_{i} to vi+1v_{i+1} for each 1≤i<n1\leq i<n, and there exists an edge directed from vnv_{n} to v1v_{1}.
•
There exist edges directed from v1v_{1} to every other vertex except vnv_{n}.
•
There exist edges directed from vjv_{j} to all vertices vkv_{k} with 1<k≤j−21<k\leq j-2.
See Figure 3 for a diagram of a useful orientation of K5K_{5}. Unless otherwise stated, all future references of KnK_{n} will refer specifically to the useful orientation of KnK_{n}.
